00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.660
what's next in the
gaming world and really

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.184
just across the board as well.

00:02:26.184 --> 00:02:27.600
To that end, we're
actually really

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.280
happy to be partnering
with Improbable

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.400
to help them develop the
SpatialOS games innovation

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.040
program.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.780
Rob, can you tell us
about how that goes?

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:35.430
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.560
So when we kind of went out
into the games community,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.018
we found there were
these fantastic

00:02:39.018 --> 00:02:41.550
ideas people had about
leveraging this amount of cloud

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.880
resource to build like the next
generation of simulated living

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.840
game worlds.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.640
And we found people
a bit apprehensive

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.340
in terms of the
cloud cost that they

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.680
might incur in terms of
their development and R&amp;D.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.510
And Google and
Improbable got together,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.070
and the innovation
program allows

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.770
us to offer like free
development costs in terms

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.052
of cloud for people from
starting development

00:03:01.052 --> 00:03:02.760
all the way through
to commercial launch.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.735
So the idea is to
sort of allow people

00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:07.110
to really kind of let loose
and experiment and try and do

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.640
some crazy things without being
worried about kind of the cost

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.220
they're going to
incur through that.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.110
MARK MANDEL: So that
sounds pretty cool.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.172
Why don't we check out
how that actually works?

00:03:18.172 --> 00:03:19.130
ROB WHITEHEAD: Awesome.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.692
So I'm going to kind
of talk on a high level

00:03:21.692 --> 00:03:23.900
about our platform as a
service, and its kind of core

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.670
architecture, and how
you think about it,

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.554
and what the abstraction is
on top of traditional servers.

00:03:28.554 --> 00:03:30.470
Then I'm going to give
you an actual live demo

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.630
on my laptop of it running
on my local machine.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.520
And then we're going to
dive into some code snippets

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.019
and kind of show
you under the hood.

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:38.000
For those of you who
have coded with Unity3D,

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.040
you might find some of
the APIs quite familiar.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.470
Who's actually used
Unity out of curiosity?

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:43.810
Great.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.990
So for those of you
who are kind of maybe

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.500
aware, traditionally when
you have online game worlds,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.060
they'll have a dedicated server.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.250
And that's a process
running inside

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:57.240
either a dedicated server
somewhere on a bare metal

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.579
hardware or on a
VM in the cloud.

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:01.120
And all the clients
within that world

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.920
connect into that single server.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.630
And this works
pretty well when you

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.490
have a small amount
of concurrent players,

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.726
or if you have a small
amount of simulation going on

00:04:08.726 --> 00:04:10.100
if your world's
not that complex.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.260
But you encounter
issues when you

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.529
want to have thousands or
tens of thousands of people

00:04:13.529 --> 00:04:15.070
in the same environment,
and you want

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.420
to have worlds that
are truly living.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.470
If you want to have
a game world which

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.720
has millions of trees
which actually grow,

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.052
in like a real ecology
and a real economy,

00:04:22.052 --> 00:04:24.010
you kind of start to hit
the fundamental limits

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.870
of a single machine no matter
how many calls you get.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.820
So the whole idea of SpatialOS
is it's a distributed operating

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.320
system.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.780
So that is to say,
it's an abstraction

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.210
on top of lots of
computers, lots of VMs.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:41.850
So you should treat it as one
big machine in some senses.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.792
And the core idea is
rather than having

00:04:43.792 --> 00:04:45.750
a single dedicated server,
which you'd normally

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.510
have, you know, Unity compiled
out as a Linux dedicated

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.120
server, or Unreal's
dedicated server,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.940
SpatialOS actually orchestrates
a swarm of dedicated servers,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.860
potentially hundreds
of thousands of them

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.720
all with overlapping
areas of interest.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.830
All kind of combined together to
simulate a massive game world.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:02.580
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.170
So basically kind of
what you're telling me

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.060
is it's kind of like
a traditional platform

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.970
as a service but just for
massive online persistent

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:09.470
worlds.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:10.200
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, exactly.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.276
And you think about--
and the whole reason

00:05:12.276 --> 00:05:13.650
we call it SpatialOS
is you think

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.330
about this big
cluster of machines

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.640
as a three dimensional
space, which

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.564
contains your computation.

00:05:19.564 --> 00:05:20.530
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.940
So as a developer then, what
does the lifecycle look like?

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.385
How am I building my stuff?

00:05:25.385 --> 00:05:25.770
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.210
So it's, again, a
traditional kind

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:28.620
of platform as a service style.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.960
You have a set of CLI
tools and web tools you

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.624
download from spatialos.com.

00:05:32.624 --> 00:05:33.540
You can do this today.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.623
You could do it right now.

00:05:34.623 --> 00:05:37.242
You should listen to
us instead, though.

00:05:37.242 --> 00:05:38.700
Once you've downloaded
those tools,

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.739
we have integrations
into Unity, into Unreal,

00:05:41.739 --> 00:05:43.530
and we also have base
language integration.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.500
So you can write code which
works in this manner in C#,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.086
C++, Java.

00:05:48.086 --> 00:05:49.710
And then you just
use some CLI commands

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.660
to package up your
application, push it

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.184
into the SpatialOS platform.

00:05:53.184 --> 00:05:54.600
And then sort of
on our side we're

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.310
going to be using
as much computation

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.934
as is necessary to
simulate your world.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.310
So these are the
kind of cool words

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.440
which you use when you're
thinking about building

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.670
something with SpatialOS.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.290
And some of you might
find some of these words

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.748
familiar, because they're very
much inspired by game engine

00:06:09.748 --> 00:06:10.740
architecture.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.170
And some of you might find
that-- like workers is

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.030
a new word we've
kind of invented,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.130
but it's actually a
broadening and an abstraction

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.213
from things you're probably
already familiar with.

00:06:19.213 --> 00:06:22.170
So in SpatialOS, the
core idea of the world

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.340
is it contains entities.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.540
It contains things.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.090
So in a video game, like you
saw "Worlds Adrift" before,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.210
each player is an entity.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.751
The rocks on the
floor are an entity,

00:06:29.751 --> 00:06:31.877
the trees, which can be
chopped down, are entities,

00:06:31.877 --> 00:06:33.542
and even the ships
are actually composed

00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:34.620
of hundreds of entities.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.110
So they really represent the
domain of your application.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.790
You can imagine if you are using
SpatialOS to simulate a city,

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.248
you might use
entities to represent

00:06:41.248 --> 00:06:44.420
road segments, buildings,
traffic signals,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.230
all kinds of different things.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.290
Then you have components.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.340
So this is like a
traditional structure

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.550
of game engine architectures.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.590
And what it lets you do
is rather than having

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:56.250
like sort of a really big,
monolithic ideas of objects,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.930
it lets you compose
them out of pieces

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:59.620
which make it a lot
easier to reason about.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:00.960
So rather than thinking
about a monster,

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.460
you think about something which
is a rigid body, which means

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.770
it can move in physical space.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.630
It has a collider, which means
it can bump into other things.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:09.550
And it has a
navigation component,

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.370
which defines the
idea of it having

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:15.062
a destination to go to and
some waypoints along the way.

00:07:15.062 --> 00:07:16.770
So the interesting
thing here is workers.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.680
Workers are-- I guess you can
think of them as microservices,

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.110
little pieces of code which
either are connecting in

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.000
over the internet, like
a game engine client,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.920
or running within
SpatialOS itself,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.440
which are authoritative
of parts of entities,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.730
as in their components.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.790
So you can imagine
taking a physics engine,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.850
like PhysX, and having that
simulates all the rigid body

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.710
components of the
entities in the world.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.270
But the magic which
is going on here

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.140
is maybe PhysX can only
simulate 10,000 rigid bodies,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.510
and your gameworld has
one million rigid bodies.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.250
What SpatialOS can do
is actually spin up

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.759
100 instances of that process,
distributed across a cluster,

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:53.300
and have them all
cooperate together.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.670
And SpatialOS is handling
all the synchronization

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.753
and all the orchestration
that's going on for you.

00:08:00.044 --> 00:08:01.710
So to kind of like
sort of bring it back

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.543
into a concrete example
based upon the video

00:08:03.543 --> 00:08:05.337
you saw with "Worlds Adrift."

00:08:05.337 --> 00:08:07.170
From their side, within
one of their worlds,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.600
the worlds are about
50 by 50 kilometers,

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.450
and you're looking at kind
of over half a million

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.640
fully physically simulated
objects inside that game.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.130
So creatures, players,
panels of a ship

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.690
can fall off and
fall onto the ground

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.146
and be completely persisted.

00:08:20.146 --> 00:08:21.520
Each one of these
islands you saw

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.960
are kind of build out of
voxels through a user generated

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.100
content tool.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.040
And those islands themselves
have about 3,000 entities,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.480
from even like an egg
on the ground or a tree,

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.030
they're all entities.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.850
And every single one of these
entries is fully persistent.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.740
So the whole idea is
SpatialOS maintains

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.720
the data representation
of the world

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.850
completely distinct from
any one of these processes

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.110
or clients connected in.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.589
So if you drop like
a sword in a bush,

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:47.130
and you kind of come
away, you should

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.094
be able to come back to
it, and years later it

00:08:49.094 --> 00:08:50.010
should still be there.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:50.884
And this is because--

00:08:50.884 --> 00:08:53.370
you can think about SpatialOS
as an in memory database

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.317
which is maintaining
all those entities.

00:08:55.317 --> 00:08:57.650
And in terms of examples of
components inside of "Worlds

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.640
Adrift," some of them are
kind of very familiar,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.181
you might find, if
you're a game dev.

00:09:01.181 --> 00:09:03.360
But within "Worlds Adrift,"
they're actually real--

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.160
like a real ecology
of creatures,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.410
and they even have
components about pregnancy

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:08.700
and how hungry they are.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.120
And it's very crazy how
much depth you can go into

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.575
to define those entities.

00:09:12.575 --> 00:09:13.950
And then in terms
of workers, you

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.180
have your workers
running in the cloud.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.460
So for "Worlds Adrift," they
have 200 Unity Linux instances

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.010
all cooperating together
to simulate the space.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.164
And they have about
1,000 Unity clients,

00:09:25.164 --> 00:09:27.330
which are players connecting
in all across the world

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:28.246
to simulate the world.

00:09:28.246 --> 00:09:31.020
So again, something massive and
far bigger than any single game

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.596
engine could deal with.

00:09:32.596 --> 00:09:33.470
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:34.562
That sounds really great.

00:09:34.562 --> 00:09:36.770
So why don't we cut to the
demo, and you can actually

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.450
show us this thing in action?

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:39.700
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.102
So this is where
it gets really fun.

00:09:42.102 --> 00:09:43.810
So it's worth saying,
you know, SpatialOS

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:44.935
is a platform as a service.

00:09:44.935 --> 00:09:47.300
It's a tool which lets
you run large scale

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.489
simulations in the cloud.

00:09:48.489 --> 00:09:50.030
But often for local
development, it's

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.520
convenient to have
a local SpatialOS.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.650
And SpatialOS can
use a single machine,

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:55.994
or it can use 1,000 machines.

00:09:55.994 --> 00:09:58.160
So we can see here as it
using just a single machine

00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.690
on my local machine.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:03.440
And we're going to be using
a tutorial which is available

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:07.179
online right now for
our Unity integration.

00:10:07.179 --> 00:10:08.470
So let's go ahead and start it.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.035
So yeah, the idea
of this sort of demo

00:10:15.035 --> 00:10:16.960
is it's like a little
diorama of SpatialOS.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.510
It's going to show you
the core concepts at work.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.130
And although we
can scale to maybe

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:23.570
hundreds of thousands times
larger than you'll see here,

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.600
it'll really kind of leave
you with the feeling of what's

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.600
going on under the hood.

00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:36.150
It's working.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.244
MARK MANDEL: Good.

00:10:37.244 --> 00:10:39.910
ROB WHITEHEAD: So what I'm going
to run here on my local machine

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:42.250
is a traditional,
built out Unity client.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:43.990
And I'm going to
be running around

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:45.692
as an awesome
little wizard, which

00:10:45.692 --> 00:10:47.650
is going to be able to
go around and set things

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:49.733
on fire, which is going
to be really, really cute.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:52.940
There we go.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.010
So, you know, this is
pretty much like sort

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:58.809
of any kind of game you
might be familiar with.

00:10:58.809 --> 00:11:00.350
And this little guy,
I can go around,

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.190
I can see things going on.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:03.740
I can set things on fire.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.930
And I can also set
the little people

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:08.970
on fire, which is not very
nice to do, but I can.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:12.330
And it's definitely
going to happen.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:12.830
I'm sorry.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.207
I missed.

00:11:17.207 --> 00:11:19.290
But you'll see it's like
just like any other game,

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.420
but already kind of
what you should see here

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.030
is the game world is actually
much bigger than what's

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.770
inside this single Unity client.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.400
The worlds actually contain
tens of thousands of trees,

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.650
and there's actually another
base very similar to this

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.400
on the other side of the world.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.130
But what's happening
is as I move around,

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.260
you kind of won't notice
it, but the subset

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:38.635
of the world I'm
interested in is

00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:41.160
being streamed in by SpatialOS.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.010
And as clients, the simulation
of those little dudes running

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.050
around isn't actually
being done on my client,

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.710
it's being done on a built
out, dedicated Unity server

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:52.597
running on my machine which
is connected into SpatialOS.

00:11:52.597 --> 00:11:54.430
And this client, rather
than being connected

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.420
into the dedicated server,
is connected into SpatialOS,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.620
and SpatialOS is managing
the interests of the world

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:00.490
as I go around.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:00.990
OK.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:02.750
I'm going to have to get
really accurate with this now.

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:03.700
Come on.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.295
No, I missed.

00:12:05.295 --> 00:12:05.920
It's all right.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.520
We can come back a little
later when there's more people.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.460
So the first thing
which I want to show you

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.256
is that's kind of what the
game client sees the world as.

00:12:14.256 --> 00:12:16.380
But I want to show you what
SpatialOS sees the game

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.520
world as.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:18.730
So this is the entire space.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:20.479
Again, it's a relatively
small simulation.

00:12:20.479 --> 00:12:23.610
It's about 10,000 entities.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.309
And already you can see that
we're actually running not just

00:12:26.309 --> 00:12:28.350
a single dedicated server,
but there are actually

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:31.276
two dedicated servers running
and overlapping together

00:12:31.276 --> 00:12:33.900
to simulate a world kind of much
bigger than a single one could

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.852
deal with for an
extended period of time.

00:12:35.852 --> 00:12:37.560
And if you zoom in
here, you can actually

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.930
see this overlapped area.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.430
And what this is
showing you as well

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.566
is the canonical
data representation

00:12:43.566 --> 00:12:44.940
of the entities
inside the world.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:46.710
So although Unity
sees them when you

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.290
log in as a client as
these really high fidelity,

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.840
graphical assets, you can see
here I can go ahead and click

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.670
and this is a tree.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.950
And if I go ahead and
like sort of click on it,

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:00.560
it can actually show me the
components of that entity.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.054
And this is really important
having this agnostic

00:13:03.054 --> 00:13:05.470
representation of the world,
because we don't care whether

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.010
the game engines and dedicated
servers have been written

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:12.310
in C#, or C++, or Java, or
whether you've used Unity

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:12.830
or Unreal.

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:16.150
This is this kind of canonical,
agnostic representation

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:18.246
of everything going on.

00:13:18.246 --> 00:13:20.370
So we can already start to
do some fun things which

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:22.440
are really scary to do
on stage but actually

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:23.520
work out pretty well.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.620
So one thing which
happens when you're

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.670
running not two but 200
or 2,000 game engines

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.810
is in the cloud
sometimes they'll crash.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.267
Code has a mean time to failure.

00:13:33.267 --> 00:13:35.100
So if I go ahead and
pull the power cord out

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.110
of one of these
workers, what's going

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.670
to happen is if you
actually zoom in,

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:41.370
you'll find the top right hand
side of the world is actually

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.200
still being simulated, but the
bottom left is actually paused.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.380
It's almost like a
glitch in the matrix.

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:50.380
But if I zoom out, you'll
find that SpatialOS detected

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:53.610
the failure, and it's actually
started up a new worker

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.610
to replace it in this
top left hand corner.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.734
And what we're showing off
here is our dynamic load

00:13:57.734 --> 00:13:59.950
balancing algorithms whereby
two workers can actually

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:02.860
change their spatial area
of interest in real time

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.760
to deal with
fluctuations of load.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:08.740
So if you have a really big
epicenter of combat going on,

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.890
you can actually have a
worker move to that area space

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:16.690
and shrink in and kind of
focus on what's happening.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:17.650
Cool.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:21.040
Let's go back and see if there's
more NPCs to have fun with.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.570
Yeah, there's a lot more.

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:24.417
Oh, no.

00:14:24.417 --> 00:14:25.469
Oh, no.

00:14:25.469 --> 00:14:26.510
That's a really bad idea.

00:14:28.855 --> 00:14:29.480
It's all right.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:30.700
One of my friends put me out.

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:32.270
Clearly nicer than me.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:35.510
The other thing to show you here
as well is if I zoom in here,

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:37.000
this is actually
showing the subset

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:41.750
of the world which the client
is actually connected into.

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:44.630
And the little blue dots, if
you can see it, is my player.

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.600
And as I move around
the world, SpatialOS

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:50.720
is understanding the subset of
the world I'm interested in.

00:14:53.444 --> 00:14:57.330
And it's going to go ahead and
move like a window of interest

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:58.824
within my world and streaming.

00:14:58.824 --> 00:15:00.240
So this is again
demonstrating you

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.830
can have game
worlds substantially

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:03.690
larger than any single
instance of Unity

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:05.640
could ever deal with or
fit inside of memory.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.040
And as you move
around, it's actually

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:10.710
streaming in just a subset of
the world you're interested in,

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.668
and it's going be keeping
you up to date there.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:18.720
Cool.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.303
So that pretty much
concludes the demo

00:15:20.303 --> 00:15:24.020
if we want to dive into
some code examples.

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:26.150
MARK MANDEL: If we can
switch back, please.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:26.890
Thank you.

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.440
All right, that
sounds really great.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.481
So yeah, let's have a look
at some code examples,

00:15:30.481 --> 00:15:33.050
and you can talk us through
how the actual coding

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:35.071
aspect of SpatialOS works.

00:15:35.071 --> 00:15:36.320
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.861
So the first thing
to talk about here

00:15:37.861 --> 00:15:39.890
is the SpatialOS
schema language.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:41.840
So I talked before
about SpatialOS

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.990
having a kind of canonical data
representation of the world,

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:47.690
which is completely distinct
of any language or any kind

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:50.850
of client or any kind of
server, like Microservice,

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.990
or kind of game
engine connected in.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:54.540
And this is how you define that.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:57.744
So this lets you define the
abstract idea of an entity

00:15:57.744 --> 00:16:00.410
without any understanding of its
implementation, which gives you

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.990
the flexibility to use
whatever tools you want.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:04.200
So this example here--

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:07.100
I'm going to show you the
implementation of flammability

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.810
within Unity.

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:12.620
And it starts off by defining
this canonical abstract idea

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:13.569
of a component.

00:16:13.569 --> 00:16:15.110
So I'm going to call
it flammability,

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:17.360
and I'm going to give it
the idea of a property being

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:19.859
is on fire, because obviously
I want to have some state for,

00:16:19.859 --> 00:16:22.940
is this thing not
on fire or on fire?

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.850
And the thing I'm going to
add as well is a command.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.090
So commands are
SpatialOS's RPC mechanism

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:32.995
which lets two workers
communicate with each other.

00:16:32.995 --> 00:16:34.870
And I'm going to define
a command here called

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:37.670
ignites, which is if I'm
something which is on fire,

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:39.680
and I'm something
which is not on fire,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.800
I can send an ignites command to
the thing which is not on fire

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:43.797
and make it ignite.

00:16:43.797 --> 00:16:45.380
And the awesome thing
Spatial is doing

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.775
for you is it's going
to automatically root

00:16:47.775 --> 00:16:50.720
that RPC to whatever
worker is authoritative

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.010
on that component at
any point in time.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:55.730
So you had this kind of really
nice transparency going on.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:58.100
And if it turns out you're
the actual worker who's

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.724
authoritative for that command,
it's going to short circuit it,

00:17:00.724 --> 00:17:03.460
and you're going to get sort of
that efficiency already built

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:03.960
in.

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:09.460
So this first part
of the example

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:13.089
is going to be about our
implementation of the Unity

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:13.780
server code.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:15.460
So this is the
code running inside

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:19.300
of the workers you saw before--
the two overlapping processes.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.480
And those of you who kind of--
who has written Unity code

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:23.760
before in C#?

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:24.260
Any chance?

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:25.000
Yeah.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.208
So you'll see something here
which is quite familiar,

00:17:27.208 --> 00:17:28.810
which is a Unity
monitor behavior.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:31.210
And the thing which is cool
and the thing which is new

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.000
is that first line where
you see require flammability

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:34.990
right there.

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.890
So SpatialOS's SDK has a really
powerful co-generation tool

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:41.650
built in, which takes
those components you

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:43.500
defined in the SpatialOS
schema language

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:47.020
and generates idiomatic APIs
in whatever kind of platform

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:50.440
you're writing clients or
dedicated server processes for.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:52.740
So in Unity, it's going
to go ahead and write

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:57.070
these classes which demonstrate
the ability to read and write

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.520
from a particular component.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:01.037
In Unreal, we'll actually
generate you act to components,

00:18:01.037 --> 00:18:03.370
and you'll be able to use the
blueprint visual scripting

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:06.250
tool to actually wire up
and do things that way.

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:08.829
Or if you are kind of writing
like a small C# microservice

00:18:08.829 --> 00:18:10.870
or something in Java,
you're going to get, again,

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:13.350
some sort of relatively
straightforward APIs,

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.240
which you can use to hook
into the different parts

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:17.637
of an entity.

00:18:17.637 --> 00:18:19.720
And the first thing I'm
going to do in this sample

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:23.600
here is add in an
implementation for ignites.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.920
So this is saying when
someone tells me to ignite,

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:29.653
here is an implementation
of the call back to call.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.780
So this is the next bit of code.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.440
So those of you who kind of
aren't familiar with Unity,

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.640
it has an idea of a trigger.

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.682
So this is a collider which
you can actually walk through.

00:18:43.682 --> 00:18:45.265
But when you walk
through and into it,

00:18:45.265 --> 00:18:47.890
it's going to fire an event
to tell you that somebody's

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:48.884
kind of gone into it.

00:18:48.884 --> 00:18:50.800
And this is a really
good tool inside of Unity

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:52.840
for dealing with
objects kind of moving

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.580
within proximity of
each other and you

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:56.000
getting alerted of that.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.500
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.500
to use the trigger
system of Unity

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.780
and kind of tie
that into SpatialOS.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.660
So that when somebody moves
into my trigger region,

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:06.700
I'm going to check--
am I on fire?

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.530
And if I'm on fire,
I'm going to send

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.080
the thing that moved into
my trigger region a command.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:13.392
And that command is
either going to--

00:19:13.392 --> 00:19:14.940
what SpatialOS is
going to do for you

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:16.830
is it's going to work out--

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:20.090
is that entity on
my worker locally?

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:21.220
Am I authoritative for it?

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.020
Then I'm going to invoke
the command locally.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:25.240
If it's not, it's going to
send it through SpatialOS,

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:26.320
and it's going to
work out whatever

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.870
worker across the cluster is
authoritative for that entity,

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:30.972
and it's going to
invoke the RPC.

00:19:30.972 --> 00:19:32.680
And it's worth noting
here I didn't write

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:34.330
any kind of serialization code.

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:36.310
I didn't write any data
synchronization code.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:37.420
It's all handled for you.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:38.920
You just kind of
invoke this method,

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:41.378
and we're going to choose the
most appropriate thing to do.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:48.120
And the final thing to see
here is the ignite handler.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:54.000
So this is when me, myself, I've
received an RPC from somebody

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:55.230
to make myself ignite.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:58.200
And this is the implementation
I've defined in Unity code

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.795
on the dedicated
server, the handler.

00:19:59.795 --> 00:20:02.295
And what you can see I'm going
to be doing here is checking,

00:20:02.295 --> 00:20:03.870
am I not on fire?

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:07.560
If I'm not on fire, I'm going
to send a new update which

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:08.740
sets me on fire.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:12.060
So when you do that dot
send, what's going to happen

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:14.850
is that dedicated
server worker is

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:18.210
going to send a message
into SpatialOS for you.

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:19.890
And SpatialOS is
going to work out

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:23.190
exactly what other workers need
to know about that property

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:23.790
update.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.680
So maybe there are
1,000 clients all

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:28.030
watching this tree, like,
really excited for it

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:29.820
to spontaneously set on fire.

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:34.584
And when that-- pyromaniacs
everywhere-- when it ignites,

00:20:34.584 --> 00:20:36.000
SpatialOS is going
to do that kind

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.180
of distributed
synchronization across all

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:39.180
these different workers.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.612
And those workers might be
running on different machines

00:20:41.612 --> 00:20:42.320
across the world.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:44.111
They might be running
on different machines

00:20:44.111 --> 00:20:44.790
in the cloud.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.310
We handle that kind of big
synchronization effort.

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.890
And you didn't write any
network synchronization code.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.830
You didn't write any
kind of sort of--

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:55.170
any kind of reliable
UDP stuff going on.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:56.859
We're using like sort
of best in class,

00:20:56.859 --> 00:20:59.400
like sort of transpose stuff to
go ahead and do that for you.

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:04.450
And then this is kind
of the fun bit, I guess,

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:07.750
which is although, SpatialOS,
one of the core ideas of it

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:11.260
is that it maintains for every
component of every entity there

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.990
is only ever one worker which
is authoritative for it, which

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:16.000
really helps in terms
of race conditions

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.200
and that kind of thing.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.180
There may be
arbitrarily many workers

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:20.597
interested in
reading a component.

00:21:20.597 --> 00:21:22.846
So you could imagine there
being thousands of clients,

00:21:22.846 --> 00:21:24.670
again, like, interested
in that tree.

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:28.450
And what you use in this
case is rather than injecting

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.630
in a flammability
reader writer interface,

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.050
you inject in a reader.

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:34.550
And this reader is
going to give you

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:38.890
callbacks for being
told when the component

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:40.840
data of that entity is updated.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:42.490
So what I've done
here is when I enable

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:45.350
I'm adding on a
visualization update.

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:49.270
So I'm going to make
a callback for when

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:53.284
this object either becomes on
fire or ceases to be on fire.

00:21:53.284 --> 00:21:54.700
And then in classic
Unity fashion,

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.550
I've got a Particle system,
which I've kind of like sort of

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:58.460
got wired up here.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:00.460
You can imagine it being
inside of a game object

00:22:00.460 --> 00:22:01.660
inside of Unity.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.510
And based upon the
abstract idea of SpatialOS,

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:07.160
it's flamability
being on fire and not

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:09.460
on fire, I'm going to go
ahead and actually utilize

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:13.510
that with a Particle system
being enabled and disabled.

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:14.969
And this is a very
nice decoupling.

00:22:14.969 --> 00:22:16.593
You might be familiar
with it if you're

00:22:16.593 --> 00:22:18.580
kind of from the webdev
world of your model

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:20.260
being decoupled from your view.

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:23.230
And this is really nice, because
this flames visualizer is

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:24.860
the one which is used on trees.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:28.240
You can imagine the one which
is being used for NPCs with them

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:30.625
kind of flailing around
their hands in a really cute

00:22:30.625 --> 00:22:34.300
and terrifying fashion is
a different piece of code.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:36.630
So it lets you kind of really
nicely compartmentalize

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.520
the implementation
of the components

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:41.530
from the visualizations
which might take place

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:43.060
on different kinds of clients.

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:44.800
And you can even
imagine the visualizer

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:46.660
code on a mobile device,
which is connected

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:50.560
into the same world, might
use like a sprite rather

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:51.852
than a full on Particle system.

00:22:51.852 --> 00:22:52.601
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:52.601 --> 00:22:54.490
So you showed us a
bunch of Unity code.

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:56.247
Just so we're all
aware as well--

00:22:56.247 --> 00:22:57.080
this works on Unity.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:59.252
This works on Unreal,
even on custom engines.

00:22:59.252 --> 00:23:00.460
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, exactly.

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.560
So as I said before,
the APIs we generate,

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.250
we have really, really
deep integrations

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.090
into Unreal and
Unity, which lets

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.350
you have these really
nice idiomatic interfaces.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.933
But if you want to write to some
microservice to do something,

00:23:11.933 --> 00:23:15.460
like say, a star pathfinding
for your NPCs moving around,

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.270
you can use our low layered
language worker integration

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:20.230
instead.

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:22.370
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:26.000
All right, so we are here
at Cloud Next, obviously.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.054
So not only is it great to
hear from you about SpatialOS

00:23:29.054 --> 00:23:30.470
and how it works,
but I'm guessing

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.803
there's some people in the
audience who may want to hear

00:23:32.803 --> 00:23:34.130
about why you're with us.

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:36.840
Why are you with
Google Platform?

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:37.340
Excellent.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.220
So I know you use a few
different products of ours

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:43.400
on Google Platform, the first
of which is Compute Engine.

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.220
So I know from my perspective,
developer advocacy,

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:49.965
quite often I speak a lot
about the cool new shiny stuff

00:23:49.965 --> 00:23:51.590
or the stuff that
automatically scales,

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:53.870
like F Engine or
something like Kubernetes.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:57.170
But you use a lot of virtual
machines on Compute Engine.

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:59.129
So if you're not familiar,
like Compute Engine,

00:23:59.129 --> 00:24:00.586
those are the
virtual machines that

00:24:00.586 --> 00:24:01.910
run on Google Cloud Platform.

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:02.710
You spin them up,
spin them down.

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:04.510
They're very traditional
in what they use.

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:06.330
Can you talk about your
experience using those?

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:07.580
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.200
So for clarity,
SpatialOS itself is

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:10.700
a platform as a
service, which means

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:13.280
you have an account
with the SpatialOS

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:16.214
and you use those CLI tools
to deploy it to the cloud.

00:24:16.214 --> 00:24:17.630
But under the hood,
we're actually

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:21.050
using Google Cloud's
actual infrastructure

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.060
to be able to run and
scale your simulation.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:25.760
And when we were choosing
cloud providers to run on,

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.800
we found that Google
Cloud Platform

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:29.360
had some favorable
characteristics

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:31.310
for certain kinds
of things we use.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:33.800
So when I talked about "Worlds
Adrift" running before,

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:37.760
it's actually running about
500 cores worth of compute

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:39.470
across about 50 machines.

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:42.310
And they're actually good--

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:44.690
VMs running on Google
Compute Engine.

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:47.174
And when you type in spatial
deploy, which is the command

00:24:47.174 --> 00:24:49.340
to actually go and start
your simulation, what we're

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:53.320
doing under the hood is
actually starting Google GCE VMs

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:56.120
to actually be running
the power and the kind

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:58.610
of the workhorse of all the
simulation going on here.

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:01.910
So the thing we actually found
with Compute Engine, which

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:04.200
made it really good for
our use cases, is we

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.715
want small teams to be able
to iterate really fast.

00:25:06.715 --> 00:25:08.840
And we want you to come up
with a really crazy idea

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.964
for a multiplayer
game, write some code,

00:25:10.964 --> 00:25:12.380
and then just run
it in the cloud,

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:13.660
and then share a link
with your friends,

00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:15.380
and then you can all
log in and play it.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:18.020
And the provisioning
time of VM, which

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:22.242
is the time you say, hey, I
want a VM to here is your VM,

00:25:22.242 --> 00:25:24.450
is a really important part
of that iteration process,

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.200
especially when you're
working with teams.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:27.992
So the awesome thing
about GCE for us

00:25:27.992 --> 00:25:29.450
is the provisioning
times, which we

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.801
found to be quite favorable
compared to other cloud

00:25:31.801 --> 00:25:32.300
providers.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:35.074
And what that means for you
as a SpatialOS developer is

00:25:35.074 --> 00:25:36.740
you've got that faster
turn around time,

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:38.220
and you can experiment quicker.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:38.970
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:40.970
And I think you were also
looking at custom VMs.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:43.250
So if you're not actually
familiar with those either,

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:45.260
Google Cloud Platform
gives you the ability

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:48.810
to create VMs with exactly
the same number of CPUs--

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:51.320
exactly the number of CPUs
and memory that it is you

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:51.862
want to use.

00:25:51.862 --> 00:25:53.570
And that works very
well for you as well.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:54.080
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah.

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.496
It's just something we're kind
of investigating right now.

00:25:56.496 --> 00:25:59.400
When you imagine running a game
engine like Unity in the cloud,

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.290
you want to make
sure that you don't

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:02.030
have any memory you're
not really using

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:03.600
or any CPU you're not using.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:06.980
And from that side, if we can
better pack Unity instances

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:09.240
on to VMs, it's
cheaper for us to run,

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:11.690
which means we can make
it cheaper for you to use.

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:12.770
And that's a very
powerful tool there,

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.230
especially because game engines
are quite weird beasts when

00:26:15.230 --> 00:26:16.730
they run in the
cloud, and they have

00:26:16.730 --> 00:26:19.320
quite strange kind of resourcing
requirements sometimes.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:21.327
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:26:21.327 --> 00:26:23.910
So when you and I got together
and started talking about this,

00:26:23.910 --> 00:26:25.830
we also started talking about
the Google Cloud Platfrom

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:26.597
Network.

00:26:26.597 --> 00:26:28.930
Which I don't know if we
necessarily talk about as much,

00:26:28.930 --> 00:26:32.400
but I think it's really
cool and interesting.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:33.960
Obviously or possibly
not obviously,

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:35.334
we run on the same
infrastructure

00:26:35.334 --> 00:26:38.915
as, say, Maps, and Gmail, and
all the other Google services.

00:26:38.915 --> 00:26:41.040
And as you may have heard
from all the stuff that's

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:42.990
been going on around
Spanner, the network

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.120
that runs Google
Cloud Platform enables

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:48.270
us to do some really cool stuff
and is exceptionally fast.

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:51.540
I think you've had a pretty
similar experience running

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:53.340
your stuff on the Google
Platforms Network.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:54.180
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, indeed.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.346
So the first thing about
real time multiplayer games

00:26:56.346 --> 00:26:59.910
which is quite different than
traditional applications is

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:02.700
rather than kind of using TCP
as the underlying transport,

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:04.470
a lot of them are
based around UDP.

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:07.830
And that's because when you're
receiving a firehose of, say,

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:10.230
60 updates a second
of a player position,

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.180
if you drop some
of those packets,

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:14.760
TCP is going to go ahead
and go, hold on, hold on.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.718
And, you know, you're
going to get these delays

00:27:16.718 --> 00:27:19.470
whilst it tries to reassemble
and give you a reliable stream.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:22.720
But with a player position, I'd
rather get the next position

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:23.760
if I miss one.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:27.060
And UDP's a natural kind of
choice of transport for that.

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:29.636
And it's basically best
in class for the industry.

00:27:29.636 --> 00:27:31.510
And what ends up happening
is people end up--

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:33.900
there's really popular
open source libraries,

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:36.540
like RakNet, which are
abstractions on top of UDP

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:38.002
to give you semi-reliability.

00:27:38.002 --> 00:27:39.960
So you can imagine their
position update of one

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.418
of those panels falling down
in "Worlds Adrift" is actually

00:27:42.418 --> 00:27:45.570
being sent unreliably with UDP.

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:48.090
Something like one of
those RPCs to ignite

00:27:48.090 --> 00:27:49.260
is actually sent reliably.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.380
So RakNet lets
you kind of have--

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:55.560
you can turn the dial from sort
of fully unreliable to fully

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:57.060
reliable.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:28:00.030
And in terms of how that applies
to Google and it's networking--

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.950
cloud providers, I
guess, traditionally

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:03.960
were really built around
scaling web services.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:08.040
So I've got some SAST Facebook
for dogs or something,

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:11.010
and I want to kind of scale
out the different 10 web

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:12.450
servers which are hosting that.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:14.910
And when we went ahead
and tried to start

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:17.940
using cloud providers for
UDP, especially like firehoses

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:19.770
of UDP packets,
you'd find there are

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:21.510
limitations in terms of the--

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:23.440
especially with
small UDP packets--

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:26.460
how many of them you can send
per second on a single VM.

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.290
And when we were kind
of profiling around,

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:32.117
we found Google's networking
had a really solid kind

00:28:32.117 --> 00:28:34.200
of performance in terms
of being able to, I think,

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.950
send like hundreds of
thousands of packets

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:37.059
a second from a single VM.

00:28:37.059 --> 00:28:38.600
And that really
translates into being

00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:41.730
able to have richer, bigger,
more interesting worlds

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.490
and have more players packed
onto a single server, which

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.010
is an important part of
having really dense combat

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:48.892
and really interesting gameplay.

00:28:48.892 --> 00:28:50.350
And yeah, it's
worth saying as well

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:53.950
that we use like sort of
clusters all over the world.

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:56.816
So we have clusters in the US.

00:28:56.816 --> 00:28:57.940
We have clusters in Europe.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.070
We have clusters in Asia.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:01.360
And they're running on
Google Compute Machines,

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:03.250
but people are connecting
over the internet

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:04.916
using Google's
networking infrastructure

00:29:04.916 --> 00:29:07.275
to connect into those
SpatialOS deployments.

00:29:09.775 --> 00:29:11.650
MARK MANDEL: Now,
something we don't probably

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:14.440
talk about that often
necessarily in the game space

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:16.840
is storage, right?

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:19.392
So you also do really neat
stuff with Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:19.392 --> 00:29:21.850
So again, if you're not familiar
with Google Cloud Storage,

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.160
this is the place for
you to store binary files

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:25.610
on our system.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.520
The nice thing about
it is that we have

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:28.960
nice things like edge caching.

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:30.980
You can store a
petabytes of data on it.

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:33.070
It'll do regional and
multiregional replication

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.439
for you out of the box.

00:29:34.439 --> 00:29:35.980
So it comes with a
lot of good stuff,

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:38.300
but I've seen you use it in
kind of interactive ways.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:39.595
It kind of works really nicely.

00:29:39.595 --> 00:29:41.178
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah,
it's really cool.

00:29:41.178 --> 00:29:43.440
So you saw before what
SpatialOS sees a game world as.

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:46.990
It sees it as just dots in
a kind of arbitrary three

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:48.250
dimensional space.

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:50.620
But when you're kind
of walking in your game

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:52.420
and you encounter an
object, maybe it's

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:54.430
an object you actually
haven't seen before.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.400
SpatialOS doesn't
actually contain

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:00.490
in its in-memory representation
the actual mesh data and sort

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:02.860
of the assets which
represent the object.

00:30:02.860 --> 00:30:06.130
All it contains is a pointer
into Google Cloud Storage

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:09.220
which contains the actual Unity
asset bundle you download.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:11.410
And this makes a ton of
sense because SpatialOS

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:14.410
is optimized for high
amounts of data throughput.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:17.790
It's optimized for
components updating maybe

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:19.182
hundreds of times a second.

00:30:19.182 --> 00:30:20.890
We don't want to be
storing kind of blobs

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:23.130
of megabytes or gigabytes of
data in that thing as well.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:24.130
It doesn't make sense.

00:30:24.130 --> 00:30:27.170
So we kind of have this tandem
thing which makes sense.

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:29.790
So when you kind of fly around
in a ship in "Worlds Adrift,"

00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:31.790
and you see an island
you haven't seen before.

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:33.370
What's going to
happen is SpatialOS

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:36.670
will say, oh, that island
is called island 4709.

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:41.500
And then using SpatialOS's APIs,
you can actually hook into our,

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:43.087
we call it our
Assembly Service, which

00:30:43.087 --> 00:30:45.170
is going to fetch to you
from Google Cloud Storage

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:48.550
the appropriate island for you
to download as an asset bundle,

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:51.020
stream it in real time
using your game engine,

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:53.070
and spawn into the game.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.364
And when we were
kind of choosing GCS,

00:30:55.364 --> 00:30:57.030
traditionally you
kind of think that you

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:59.020
need to use a CDN for
this kind of thing.

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:01.720
But we actually
profiled that GCS

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:04.290
against sort of
conventional CDN providers,

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:05.860
and we found it was comparable.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:08.250
And we were using
this anyway for how

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.110
we store a lot of
things within Spatial,

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:13.540
so it made total sense for us.

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:14.590
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:16.909
So you're using a
bunch of products

00:31:16.909 --> 00:31:18.700
from Google Cloud
Platform, which is great,

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:20.820
but you're also using some of
our open source stuff as well.

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:21.010
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah.

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:22.426
MARK MANDEL: You're
actually using

00:31:22.426 --> 00:31:24.570
one of my favorite open
source projects, GRPC.

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:28.600
So GRPC is a high performance,
open-source universal remote

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:30.320
procedure call framework.

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:32.070
It actually-- yeah,
it came out of Stubby.

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:36.010
So it came out of, historically,
our internal remote procedure

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:37.444
call framework called Stubby.

00:31:37.444 --> 00:31:38.485
It's really, really cool.

00:31:38.485 --> 00:31:39.776
It's works really, really fast.

00:31:39.776 --> 00:31:41.750
It does streaming data
really, really well.

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:44.500
It has been a huge thing around
how we talk about microservices

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:46.000
at Google and Google Cloud.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:47.090
Can you talk about how
you've been using it?

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:48.640
I think it's really
interesting for games.

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:49.890
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah, for sure.

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:53.380
So you can imagine with games
bandwidth is absolutely key

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.120
and performance
is absolutely key.

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:57.700
You don't want to be firehosing
around like JSON blobs which

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:00.846
represent entity position when
you're sending hundreds of them

00:32:00.846 --> 00:32:02.470
a second per entity,
and you might have

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:04.900
1,000 entities in your scene.

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:07.470
And also when you're
running, like Improbable,

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:10.977
we have a team of
around 100 engineers.

00:32:10.977 --> 00:32:13.060
We have a constellation
of different microservices

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:14.380
that make up SpatialOS.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:16.120
So besides the
actual runtime, which

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:18.310
is this kind of in-memory,
spatial simulation

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:20.340
representation, we
have microservices

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:22.360
for things like
accounts, billing,

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:24.580
storing deployment
snapshots of your world,

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:28.220
storing the binary assets you
download, like your trees,

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:29.170
et cetera.

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:30.910
And we reached a
point where we were

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:34.490
using kind of a restful
interface for all those things.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:36.544
But you start to
have a lot of issues

00:32:36.544 --> 00:32:38.710
arise, because you don't
have a typed representation

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:39.870
of that interface,
and you sort of

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:41.800
have teams like stepping
on each other's toes.

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:44.110
And you have to rely on
large amounts of unit testing

00:32:44.110 --> 00:32:45.568
and integration
testing to actually

00:32:45.568 --> 00:32:48.200
make sure your software works.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:49.890
But under the hood
for us, we used

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.660
GRPC for all of our
microservice architecture.

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:56.170
And actually with Spacial, if
you type in spatial deploy,

00:32:56.170 --> 00:33:00.080
that's actually using GRPC
calls to talk to our platform

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:02.364
to kick off that deployment.

00:33:02.364 --> 00:33:04.780
And another thing we actually
have in the pipeline as well

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:08.160
is that inspector you saw,
that kind of WebGL accelerated

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:11.260
entity view, when you
kind of move it around it

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:13.180
actually sets up
a streaming query

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:16.220
for a subset of the worlds to be
streamed down into the browser.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.850
And again, GRPC looks like
a very powerful candidate

00:33:18.850 --> 00:33:20.810
to kind of help
accelerate that as well.

00:33:20.810 --> 00:33:22.880
And it's kind of based
on HTTP, too, as well.

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:26.295
So it's really nice for kind
of lots and lots of requests

00:33:26.295 --> 00:33:30.170
and responses really quickly and
also for streaming responses.

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:32.120
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:32.620
Sweet.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:35.250
So you're showing us some
really amazing stuff.

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:36.840
Super excited about
SpatialOS, I think

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:38.096
it's really exciting tech.

00:33:38.096 --> 00:33:40.470
Can you tell us about what
plans you have for the future?

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:41.430
What can we see?

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:43.240
What's coming up
down the pipeline?

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:43.470
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah.

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:45.719
So there's kind of two
different angles to talk about.

00:33:45.719 --> 00:33:49.650
So the natural first thing is
about scale and performance.

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:51.720
We really want to be
this platform where

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:54.630
you can have game worlds, or
even simulations of the living

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:56.800
world, which are a
kind of reality scale.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:59.400
And by that we mean
millions of concurrent plays

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.800
in the same environment
with high fidelity physics,

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:05.370
maybe even in VR, and
billions of simulated entities

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:07.560
which have rich AI,
they're fully physical,

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:08.880
and they're fully persistent.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.764
And all of our dev
teams at Spacial,

00:34:10.764 --> 00:34:12.389
especially on our
R&amp;D side, are focused

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:15.360
on trying to push the
capabilities of SpatialOS

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:16.384
further and further.

00:34:16.384 --> 00:34:18.300
And that really comes
down to how many clients

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:19.800
you can have in
that world, and also

00:34:19.800 --> 00:34:23.310
how many servers can participate
in this single, uncharted,

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:25.330
seamless spatial computation.

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:27.750
And I'm really excited
to see the direction we

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.659
go in the next couple of years.

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:31.159
We kind of want to
build the Matrix.

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:32.290
That's probably something we--

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:33.570
it's a story for
another time, but that's

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:35.528
kind of-- you can imagine
the foundations of it

00:34:35.528 --> 00:34:36.330
based upon that.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:37.980
And then the other side is--

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:38.900
now we're kind of--

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:42.060
friends of Google is looking
at the constellation of tools

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:44.790
and services they have and
how people could easily

00:34:44.790 --> 00:34:47.429
tie them in working
with SpatialOS to do

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:48.600
some awesome new things.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.150
So you can imagine
a game world which

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:55.679
is like a successor to a Pokemon
Go geo game, where you're

00:34:55.679 --> 00:34:58.230
using Google Maps data and
streaming that in and using

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:01.060
Google Maps objects to
represent SpatialOS entities.

00:35:01.060 --> 00:35:02.310
That could be really exciting.

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:05.670
Or on our kind of non-gaming
side, doing city simulations

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:08.299
that are actually based upon
Google Maps's sort of traffic

00:35:08.299 --> 00:35:09.840
information and
building information.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:11.680
That will be really exciting.

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:13.460
We have some sort of
interesting angles

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:15.001
we're looking at
with kind of YouTube

00:35:15.001 --> 00:35:17.635
and how streaming plays a
part in real, living worlds.

00:35:17.635 --> 00:35:19.260
And I think there's
some exciting stuff

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:21.120
we can talk about soon on that.

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:25.290
And then, again, another kind
of Skynet-esque kind of thing

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:28.380
to think about is integrating
in technologies like TensorFlow

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:29.310
into Spatial.

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:31.650
So I talked before
about the workers

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:34.300
that connect into SpatialOS
can be completely arbitrary.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:36.960
So I had a hackathon
team a few weeks ago,

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:39.930
and they actually integrated a
C++ TensorFlow worker running

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:42.810
in interactive mode into
SpatialOS and was doing

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:45.840
reinforcement learning of these
little entities kind of moving

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:47.910
around and eating things
inside of Spatial.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:50.070
And they were kind of
sharing the same brain.

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:52.154
So as you had multiple
entities in the same world,

00:35:52.154 --> 00:35:53.695
they were actually
combining together

00:35:53.695 --> 00:35:54.960
their learning information.

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:57.060
And I think the
idea of SpatialOS

00:35:57.060 --> 00:36:00.360
is almost like a kindergarten
or a sandbox for AIs

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:03.540
to learn about physical systems
and learn how to interact

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:06.120
is a really exciting
proposal as well.

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:07.280
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:07.780
All right.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:09.040
Well, we're going to
have plenty of time

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:10.540
for questions, which is great.

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:13.270
First of all, Rob, thank you so
much for joining us on stage.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:15.970
But before we go, do you
have any final thoughts,

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.190
or like, the one
thing the people need

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:20.440
to remember about SpatialOS
before they end up

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:21.180
leaving the room?

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:21.400
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah.

00:36:21.400 --> 00:36:22.990
It's not crazy magic technology.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:25.960
You can literally go to
spatialos.com right now.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:27.820
You can download the SDK.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:29.200
You can play around with it.

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.825
In terms of what you can use it
for, you can use it with Unity,

00:36:31.825 --> 00:36:33.949
and you can sort of be
running things in the cloud.

00:36:33.949 --> 00:36:35.380
You can be using Unreal.

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:38.076
And yeah, you can also be
using like whatever language

00:36:38.076 --> 00:36:39.700
or whatever tools
you're familiar with.

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:42.910
They're very easy to integrate
into SpatialOS's representation

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:43.936
of the world.

00:36:43.936 --> 00:36:45.310
And in terms of
the two things we

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:47.920
want to let people do with
Spatial on the gaming side--

00:36:47.920 --> 00:36:49.974
one is teams, no
matter how big or small

00:36:49.974 --> 00:36:51.640
they are, being able
to do fundamentally

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.520
new kinds of things
in the game industry.

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:55.847
So game worlds which
are truly persistent

00:36:55.847 --> 00:36:57.430
have millions of
living objects inside

00:36:57.430 --> 00:36:58.930
of them, tens of
thousands of people

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:00.470
inside of them simultaneously.

00:37:00.470 --> 00:37:01.720
We want to make that possible.

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:04.240
And also because SpatialOS
is a platform as a service,

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:06.280
we want to take small
teams, teams like Bossa,

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:08.160
who don't really have
an infrastructure team.

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:09.660
They don't have
network programmers.

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:11.950
They don't have all the
people you traditionally

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.527
expect you need to
build persistent worlds.

00:37:14.527 --> 00:37:15.360
They're going ahead.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:17.590
They're picking up SpatialOS
and its Unity integration,

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:19.210
and they're doing things
which really they'd

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:20.410
find like fundamentally
impossible,

00:37:20.410 --> 00:37:22.360
or they'd need to hire
beyond their means

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.290
to make it possible.

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:25.540
So it's about everybody being
able to do fundamentally

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:27.460
new kinds of things,
and small teams

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:30.010
being able to go further without
any specialist techniques

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:30.659
and tools.

00:37:30.659 --> 00:37:32.200
MARK MANDEL: Building
the next games.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:33.280
ROB WHITEHEAD: Building-- yeah.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:34.488
MARK MANDEL: I just, I can't.

00:37:34.488 --> 00:37:35.460
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:36.520
OK, it's terrible.

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:37.130
I know.

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:37.630
Excellent.

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:41.560
So if people want to learn more
about SpatialOS, spatialos.com,

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:42.710
like you mentioned.

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:44.600
The GitHub has-- the GitHub--

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:47.050
GitHub has the
Hello World sample.

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:48.460
They're available for you to do.

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:50.470
If people want to reach out
to you on Twitter or email?

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:51.970
ROB WHITEHEAD: Yeah,
yeah, go ahead.

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:54.430
The forums of SpatialOS are
really active and definitely

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:55.570
worth checking out.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:57.760
And the demo I ran
through today is actually

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:01.030
the exact demo you use when
you sign up for SpatialOS.

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:03.490
You get access, and you kind
of go through your first kind

00:38:03.490 --> 00:38:04.780
of Hello World tutorial.

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:05.950
You'll be running
through exactly what we

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:07.390
ran through here,
but the next step

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:09.473
is deploying it onto the
cloud and then sharing it

00:38:09.473 --> 00:38:10.287
with people.

00:38:10.287 --> 00:38:11.245
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:38:11.245 --> 00:38:12.979
Well, so if anyone
has any questions,

00:38:12.979 --> 00:38:14.770
there is a microphone
standing right there.

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:16.360
So if you could come
up to it, and if you

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:18.776
could ask your questions so I
can get it on the recording,

00:38:18.776 --> 00:38:19.840
that would be great.

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:21.650
But otherwise, thank
you very much to Rob.

00:38:21.650 --> 00:38:23.380
ROB WHITEHEAD: Thank you
very much for having me.

00:38:23.380 --> 00:38:24.963
MARK MANDEL: Do we
have any questions?

